To find the sum of the y-coordinates of the other two vertices, we need to find the midpoint of the diagonal of the square, which will be the midpoint of the other two vertices.

The midpoint of the diagonal can be found using the midpoint formula:

Midpoint = ((x1 + x2)/2, (y1 + y2)/2)

Given points: (7,9) and (10,2)

Midpoint = ((7 + 10)/2, (9 + 2)/2)
Midpoint = ((17)/2, (11)/2)
Midpoint = (8.5, 5.5)

Since the midpoint of the diagonal is (8.5, 5.5), the other two vertices will be equidistant from this point. 

The distance between the midpoint and the given points is the same as the distance between the midpoint and the other two vertices.

The distance between (7,9) and (8.5, 5.5) is:
√((7-8.5)^2 + (9-5.5)^2) = √((-1.5)^2 + (3.5)^2) = √(2.25 + 12.25) = √14.5

The distance between (10,2) and (8.5, 5.5) is:
√((10-8.5)^2 + (2-5.5)^2) = √((1.5)^2 + (-3.5)^2) = √(2.25 + 12.25) = √14.5

Since the distance is the same, the other two vertices will be at the same distance from the midpoint in the vertical direction.

The vertical distance between the midpoint (8.5, 5.5) and the given points is:
|9-5.5| = 3.5

Since the other two vertices are at the same distance from the midpoint in the vertical direction, their y-coordinates will be 3.5 units above and below the midpoint.

The y-coordinates of the other two vertices are:
5.5 + 3.5 = 9
5.5 - 3.5 = 2

The sum of the y-coordinates of the other two vertices is:
9 + 2 = 11

\boxed{11}

